# An Efficient Quantum Circuit for Block Encoding a Pairing Hamiltonian

Diyi Liu, Weijie Du, Lin Lin, James P. Vary, Chao Yang

arXiv:2402.11205v3 [nucl-th] 22 Feb 2024

## Extraction Note

This is a placeholder / stub for the Nougat extraction of the paper. The
canonical full-text used during replication is `work/2402.11205.txt`
(pdftotext dump of the arXiv v3 PDF at `work/2402.11205.pdf`), which was
sufficient for section-level cross-reference while coding the verification.

A full Nougat OCR run (`nougat 2402.11205.pdf`) was not required for this
replication because (a) all central numerical claims (Eq. 41, Sec. 5.2.2
$(16, 5)$-block encoding, Sec. 4.4 gate-count formulas) were unambiguous
in the pdftotext output, and (b) the replication is an independent
reimplementation of the algorithm rather than a value-extraction exercise.

## Key Sections Referenced During Replication

- **Sec. 3 (Def. 3.1):** definition of $(\alpha, m, \varepsilon)$-block
  encoding of a Hamiltonian $H$.
- **Sec. 4 (Thm. 4.1):** Camps--Grover--Rossi--Van~Beeumen decomposition
  $U_H = D \cdot O_C \cdot D^\dagger \cdot O_H$ for sparse-matrix
  block-encoding.
- **Sec. 4.1.3:** pair-operator commutation removes Jordan--Wigner phases.
- **Sec. 4.4:** closed-form gate counts $12L\log L + 23L$ two-qubit,
  $14L\log L + 21L$ $T$.
- **Sec. 5.2 (Table 1, Eq. 39, Eq. 41):** 3-nucleon / 6-single-particle
  worked example; explicit $9\times 9$ integer $H_{\text{pair}}|_{M_J=+1/2}$.
- **Sec. 5.2.2:** headline claim $(\alpha, m) = (16, 5)$.
- **Sec. 5.3:** QSVT application to density of states (out of scope for
  this replication).
- **Sec. 6:** extension to general 2nd-quantized Hamiltonians (qualitative,
  out of scope).

